# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# refund-api environment variables
#
# DATA RESIDENCY — MANDATORY constraints (spec 007, CLAUDE.md):
#   1. EU region: this service MUST be deployed to an EU region only.
#      Production target: Railway EU (europe-west4), matching estimai-api/
#      notify-api's railway.json — `refund-api/railway.json` + `Dockerfile`
#      (T19) pin the same region/build shape. No reimbursement data
#      (financial figures, receipt PII) may be stored outside the EU — this
#      also covers the object storage bucket below (ADR-0016).
#   2. No body logging: request/response bodies (expense line motivations,
#      amounts, receipt metadata) must NEVER appear in application logs.
#      hono/logger is configured to emit method+path+status only (see
#      src/index.ts / src/lib/logger.ts). Do NOT add any logger that includes
#      body, query parameters with content, or the Prisma 'query' log level.
#
# T4 (this bootstrap) wired identity verification (ADR-0005/0010) + local
# `bun run dev`. T19 (this pass) adds: Docker/Railway deploy config
# (Dockerfile, railway.json — EU region), the root `mise run dev`/`db:migrate`
# wiring, a `refund-api` service in compose.yaml, and documents (but does NOT
# yet schema-enforce — see each section below) the object-storage and
# notify-api-internal-call env vars that T9/T13 will consume. See
# infra/README.md for the full deploy runbook and Variable reference.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ─── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local: point at the compose Postgres (host port 5435, 'refund' database).
# Production (Railway): use the Railway-provisioned Postgres URL (Railway EU).
# T5 adds the first migration (0001_init) — this DB is otherwise empty until
# `bun run db:migrate` is run after that schema lands.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5435/refund

# ─── CORS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of allowed origins (no wildcards).
# Must include the shell origin(s) for each environment — refund-ui is a
# federated remote reached through the shell (ADR-0006), not called directly.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:3000

# ─── Auth (JWT resource-server verification) ─────────────────────────────────
# auth service JWKS endpoint — used by jwtMiddleware to verify RS256 tokens.
# Both values are required; the service will refuse to start if either is missing.
#
# IMPORTANT: use /auth/jwks (better-auth's built-in JWKS endpoint) NOT
# /.well-known/jwks.json — the latter serves the env-var key (kid:
# operai-auth-rs256-v1) which is a DIFFERENT key from the one better-auth's
# jwt plugin uses (stored in the jwks table, dynamic kid). The /auth/token
# endpoint signs with the DB keypair, so refund-api must verify against
# /auth/jwks.
AUTH_JWKS_URL=http://localhost:3001/auth/jwks
AUTH_ISSUER=http://localhost:3001

# auth service base URL — used by authzMiddleware to call GET /authz/resolve
# (the Bearer-authed resource-server authorization seam, ADR-0014). Required;
# same host as AUTH_ISSUER locally (kept distinct because issuer is a token-claim
# value while this is a fetch target). Provision identically per environment.
AUTH_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# Suite-wide JWT audience (ADR-0010). Must exactly match the `audience` claim
# the auth service stamps via its jwt plugin's definePayload hook — a mismatch
# (or a token that predates the claim) is rejected with 401. Treat this as a
# single suite-wide constant, documented identically in auth/.env.example and
# every resource server's .env.example (estimai-api, notify-api, refund-api).
AUTH_AUDIENCE=operai-suite

# ─── Server ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8082 — estimai-api is 8080, notify-api is 8081 (CLAUDE.md § Resource backends).
PORT=8082
NODE_ENV=development

# ─── Object storage (T9, ADR-0016 — ENFORCED, all five vars REQUIRED) ────────
# EU-region S3-compatible bucket for receipt attachments, consumed by
# `refund-api/src/lib/storage.ts`. All five vars are validated in
# src/lib/env.ts (`process.exit(1)` on any missing var).
#
# PROVISIONED: a Railway S3-compatible bucket in EU Amsterdam — a CUSTOM,
# non-AWS endpoint (NOT `*.amazonaws.com`). This is a deliberate deviation
# from ADR-0016's named AWS-S3/Scaleway/R2 candidate list, made at
# implementation time; still satisfies the ADR's actual constraint set (EU
# region, S3-compatible, presigned-URL support).
#
# EU-RESIDENCY GUARD IS ENDPOINT-AWARE (src/lib/s3Residency.ts): a strict
# AWS-region allowlist would WRONGLY reject this bucket, because a non-AWS
# provider's `region` string is an opaque SDK placeholder (often literally
# "us-east-1" even for a physically-EU bucket), not a real location encoding.
# The startup assertion therefore only enforces the EU-AWS-region allowlist
# when REFUND_S3_ENDPOINT's host is `*.amazonaws.com`; for a custom endpoint
# like this one, residency is OPERATOR-ASSERTED by having actually
# provisioned the bucket in Amsterdam — REFUND_S3_REGION is just required to
# be present (any value; Railway's S3-compatible API expects one, even if
# it's not geographically meaningful).
#
# PATH-STYLE ADDRESSING: storage.ts sets `forcePathStyle: true` automatically
# for any non-AWS endpoint (Railway/MinIO/Scaleway-style buckets generally
# require it) — no separate env var needed.
#
# SECURITY — THE BUCKET MUST BE PRIVATE: no public-read ACL/bucket policy.
# Every read goes through refund-api's own short-lived (~60s) presigned GET,
# minted ONLY after the ownership/entity-scope authz check passes
# (GET .../attachments/:aid/url) — the bucket itself must reject anonymous
# reads. DEPLOY CHECKLIST: verify the Railway bucket's public-access toggle
# is OFF before go-live (receipts may contain PII — names, addresses, card
# tails).
#
# Local dev: point these at the real Railway bucket via direnv/1Password
# (refund-api/.envrc) — do NOT commit real credentials here. `bun test`
# mocks the storage layer (src/lib/storage.ts is `mock.module()`-replaced in
# attachments.routes.test.ts), so it never needs a live bucket; `bun run dev`
# + a real upload attempt DOES need working credentials below.
REFUND_S3_ENDPOINT=https://your-bucket.s3.railway-eu-amsterdam.example.com
REFUND_S3_REGION=auto
REFUND_S3_BUCKET=operai-refund-attachments-dev
REFUND_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=change-me
REFUND_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=change-me

# ─── notify-api internal call (T19 wiring; ENFORCED starting T13, ADR-0011/0017) ─
# refund-api → notify-api `POST /system/notifications` after an approve/reject
# decision (plan.md "On decision → notify", ADR-0017). Same shared-secret
# pattern as auth → notify-api's existing `/system/emails` call
# (auth/.env.example, auth/src/lib/notify.ts) — NOT a user JWT/JWKS path.
#
# NOTIFY_INTERNAL_TOKEN MUST be byte-for-byte identical across auth,
# notify-api, AND refund-api (three services sharing one secret as of this
# wiring — ADR-0017 explicitly notes this trips ADR-0011's named "second
# internal caller" escalation trigger without acting on it yet). For local
# dev, generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32` and paste the SAME value into
# this file, notify-api/.env, and auth/.env's NOTIFY_INTERNAL_TOKEN. Never
# logged by any service. T13 (`refund-api/src/lib/notify.ts`) is the task that
# adds this to src/lib/env.ts's zod schema (`min(32)`, no default — the
# service will refuse to start without it once T13 lands, mirroring
# notify-api's own env.ts).
NOTIFY_INTERNAL_TOKEN=change-me-to-a-secure-random-string-shared-with-auth-and-notify-api

# notify-api's base URL for this internal call. Local: notify-api's own dev
# port (8081). Production (Railway): notify-api's PRIVATE-networking address
# (e.g. http://notify-api.railway.internal:8081), NOT its public domain — same
# posture as auth's NOTIFY_INTERNAL_URL (infra/README.md § Variable reference),
# this call should never traverse the public internet.
NOTIFY_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:8081

# ─── Not yet wired (added by later tasks — do not add manually) ──────────────
# T6  — no new env var (calls `auth GET /authz/resolve`, forwarding the
#        caller's own Bearer token; no separate credential — reuses the
#        existing AUTH_ISSUER above as the auth service's base URL, same
#        value as `auth`'s own BETTER_AUTH_URL / estimai-api's AUTH_ISSUER,
#        infra/README.md "Cross-service wiring").
